THIRD-PARTY WORK
----------------
agent/main.py is a MODIFIED copy of a public Kaggle notebook agent (the 27 September 2026 "F4" version of
"demand-preserving-turn-sale-timing"), which is itself the latest step of a chain of public notebooks.
The unmodified upstream notices, including the full Apache-2.0 text, are kept at the top of
agent/main.py and in agent/NOTICE.txt. Those notices name, among others:

  thomastschinkel (Thomas Tschinkel)  state router, Metav4, published action routes and decision trees
  yhay81 (Yusuke Hayashi)             ShopRouter routes
  Ahmed Berat Ozer                    V55/V56 controllers, input budgeting
  shiiin9, prvsiyan, destbreso, aurax7, tetsutani
  Dmitrii Gluzdov                     rescue controller, adaptation, integration

We have not tried to establish every contributor beyond what those notices say. The notices are authoritative.
No endorsement by any of them, or by Kaggle, is implied.

The four-line "snipe" change in agent/lineage/05-snipe.diff was taken from the public notebook
"a-song-of-ice-and-fire-fixed-flexible" by AlekseiProvorov. It is not our work.

tools/screen.py and the analysis scripts read the Kaggle `kaggle-environments` package (Apache-2.0, Kaggle and
the kaggle-environments contributors). next-agent/planner/main.py embeds market rules derived from the
Apache-2.0 Metav4 and F4 sources; their attribution is in the text of that file.

CHANGES WE MADE TO THE AGENT (Apache-2.0 section 4(b))
-----------------------------------------------------
Five layers were added or changed. Each is a diff in agent/lineage/:
  01 counter against a mirror opponent (appended code)
  02 race: two lines in _s809_apply
  03 wheat round-trip (appended code)
  04 look-ahead: one constant
  05 snipe: four lines (from the public notebook above)
In addition, two comment lines in agent/main.py that named ladder teams were redacted ("Team H", "Team D").
This changes no behaviour. The file submitted to Kaggle differs from agent/main.py only in those two comments.

DATA
----
No raw replays are included. Derived tables in evidence/ and analysis/ come from public Kaggle episode replays.
Top-ranked teams are anonymised as Team A-G, and other ladder opponents are replaced by hashes. Kaggle's competition rules and terms apply to the underlying replays.
